{
  "dom_id": "step-q344829",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Find the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x$ that is closest to the point $(2,-\\frac{7}{2}).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,-\\frac{7}{2})$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+{(y+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}+7y+\\frac{49}{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+{y}^{2}+7y+\\frac{65}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}-4x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =y+{y}^{2}+7y+\\frac{65}{4} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+8y+\\frac{65}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y+8 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =-8 \\\\ y & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=-4$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=-4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
